From 388522f499a9d30f481922f45fa51c4bdadb6a18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: carlosmolina0615 <carlosmolina0615@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 23:27:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Route the CED decoder's MoE with its own padding mask in
 TileLang

A CED decoder layer routes the step's rows gathered by CED's indices, not
the step's rows. The TileLang routers took the step's padding mask and
sliced it to the decoder's row count (0035), which is only right when
padding sits at the end and the plan is exact. Neither holds in a mixed
step: adaptive verification marks dead verification rows inside the batch
(0010), and the plan's CPU upper bound leaves -1 indices at its end when
verification shortens the decode ranges. The router then routed padding
rows, whose NaN logits stopped the engine at the TileKernels assertion,
and skipped live ones.

DeepseekV41ModelState.prepare_attn now gathers the step's mask by the CED
indices, with the -1 indices padding, and puts it on the decoder metadata
once per step. The decoder layers' routers are tagged at construction and
take that mask; encoder and drafter routers keep the step's. A compact
mask is never chosen by row count alone, since an encoder forward can have
the same number of rows. The mask is a new tensor each step, so a CED step
that would replay a CUDA graph is refused; CED starts above the capture
sizes.

Reproduced on three GB10 nodes with r6 (TP3, 4 KiB profile, 2.0 and
2.2 GiB KV, with and without prefix caching): a prefill with 3,328 cached
tokens admitted while five requests decode fails at the first mixed step.
With this change, two rounds of six concurrent streams completed 24 of 24
requests with prefix caching on, with no NaN or Inf.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
